feat: tiled doodle image behind the hero

Replaces the hand-authored SVG mark pattern with the supplied doodle
artwork as a tiled background. Several rounds of tuning the generated
marks never got the field to read as texture; the artwork does it
directly, and it is the client's own.

Two things tame the source. It is solid black line work at close to full
density, so the layer sits at 7% opacity: measured, the darkest the
ground reaches is about #ededed, leaving the ink headline at 16.9:1. And
it is tiled rather than stretched, because the artwork is portrait
(736x977) and the hero is landscape — fitting one to the other would
distort every mark.

Tile size is held near the source's own width, clamped 340-620px. Drawn
much smaller than the source, every mark shrinks with it and the field
reads far denser than the artwork actually is: at the 300px first tried,
marks were about a third of their drawn size and the density roughly
tripled.

The asset is resized to 560px wide and encoded as webp, 71KB against the
200KB jpeg. The layer sits above the hero's media layer, which fills the
section whether or not a media file exists and would otherwise cover
this entirely, and below the content at z-10. HeroPattern.tsx, the
--hero-mark token and the pattern's responsive rules are removed.

Verified: no failed requests, no console errors, no overflow at 390px.
